        "heading": "2.2 GRADIENT-BASED META-LEARNING METHODS",
        "text": "One highly successful meta-learning algorithm has been Model Agnostic Meta-Learning (MAML) (Finn et al., 2017), which led to the development of a series of related gradient-based metalearning (GBML) methods Raghu et al. (2019); Nichol & Schulman (2018); Antoniou et al. (2018); Hospedales et al. (2020). Although MAML does not explicitly try to learn a useful representation, in (Raghu et al., 2019) it was found that MAML tends to learn a shared representation across tasks then performing local adaptation on its final layer. Based on such observations, a modified version of MAML, known as the Almost-No-Inner-Loop algorithm (ANIL) (Raghu et al., 2019) was proposed, which freezes the representation during local adaptation. Recently, theoretical analysis (Collins et al., 2022) was provided for MAML and ANIL on multi-task linear representation learning scenarios, and found that the driving force causing MAML and ANIL to recover the underlying representation is the adaptation of the final layer of their models, which harnesses the underlying task diversity to improve the representation in all directions of interest.\nAlthough MAML and the general meta-learning approaches have achieved impressive performance in some machine-learning applications such as the image classification and reinforcement learning scenarios, a few work (Kailkhura et al., 2019; Mai et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022; Goswami et al., 2022b) has studied the hidden physics learning under meta or even transfer setting. None of these works have provided a generic approach nor theoretical understanding on how to transfer the multi-\ntask knowledge between a series of complex physical systems, such that all these tasks are governed by a common parametric PDE with different physical parameters."
